WEBVTT

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.180
<v Ira Glass>So Lilly, you're here to tell me a story?
<v Ira Glass>莉莉，你是来给我讲故事的吗？

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:02.870
<v Lilly Sullivan>Yes.
<v Lilly Sullivan>是的。

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:05.450
<v Ira Glass>And I should say, you are Lilly Sullivan. You're one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>我应该介绍一下，你是莉莉·沙利文，是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:06.800
<v Lilly Sullivan>Mm-hm, I am.
<v Lilly Sullivan>嗯，是的。

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.030
<v Ira Glass>So what's the story?
<v Ira Glass>那么，是什么故事？

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:25.220
<v Lilly Sullivan>OK, so this is a story that I heard growing up. It was one of those things I was told as like, a scary story when you're a kid. Like an urban legend. And then I've been thinking about it a lot recently, because I read a book recently that I really loved. It's called Her Body and Other Parties, by this writer, Carmen Maria Machado.
<v Lilly Sullivan>好的，这是一个我从小听到大的故事。在我小时候，它被当成那种恐怖故事来讲，就像都市传说一样。最近我经常想起它，因为我最近读了一本我很喜欢的书，书名叫《她的身体和其他派对》，作者是卡门·玛丽亚·马查多。

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:36.470
<v Lilly Sullivan>It's a finalist for the National Book Award, and she retells the story in one of the short stories in her book. And it just stuck in my head. And it's just something that I've been thinking about a lot recently.
<v Lilly Sullivan>这本书入围了国家图书奖，她在书中的一篇短篇小说里重述了这个故事。它一直在我脑海里挥之不去，也是我最近经常思考的事情。

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:37.850
<v Ira Glass>And so what happens? How does it go?
<v Ira Glass>那么发生了什么？故事是怎么发展的？

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:48.350
<v Lilly Sullivan>OK, so a girl and her mom are on vacation in Paris. And they're just traveling, and they've been there for a few days. And the mom, she gets sick. And so the girl gets a doctor.
<v Lilly Sullivan>好的，有个女孩和她妈妈在巴黎度假。她们在旅行，已经待了几天了。然后妈妈生病了，女孩就去找了医生。

00:00:48.350 --> 00:01:01.820
<v Lilly Sullivan>And the doctor comes to the hotel. He spends a while examining her, and he's like, she's really sick. We need to deal with this immediately. And he tells the daughter, you need to go across the city. I have medicine. It can save her. You have to go get it while I tend to her, sends her downstairs.
<v Lilly Sullivan>医生来到酒店，检查了一会儿，说：“她病得很重，我们必须马上处理。”他对女儿说：“你需要穿过城市去拿药，这药能救她。趁我照顾她的时候，你快去拿。”就把她打发下楼了。

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:14.630
<v Lilly Sullivan>And the guy at the front desk, the hotel manager, takes her out, and he puts her in a cab. And he goes to the taxi driver and, in French, tells him directions to where she's supposed to go. And it's on the other side of the city. So the girl is in the back of the cab.
<v Lilly Sullivan>前台的工作人员，也就是酒店经理，带她出去并让她上了出租车。他用法语告诉司机要去的地方，目的地在城市的另一头。女孩就这样坐在出租车后座。

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:29.990
<v Lilly Sullivan>And it's just taking forever. The taxi driver is making all these loops and getting lost. . And she's kind of frantic in the back, needing to get back. But she finally gets there. She gets the pills. She jumps back in the cab. And they drive back to the hotel.
<v Lilly Sullivan>路上花了很长时间。出租车司机绕来绕去，好像迷路了。她在后座很焦急，急着要回去。但她最终还是到了，拿到了药，又跳上出租车，赶回酒店。

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:43.130
<v Lilly Sullivan>She runs into the building. And she's running past the hotel manager. And he stops her. And he says, miss, can I help you? And she says, yeah, I have it. I found the medicine. I'm just going back up. He's like, I'm sorry. I've never seen you before.
<v Lilly Sullivan>她冲进大楼，跑过酒店经理时被他拦住了。他问：“小姐，有什么可以帮您吗？”她说：“有的，我有药了，我找到了，我要回楼上去。”他说：“抱歉，我以前没见过您。”

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:54.590
<v Lilly Sullivan>And she's like, no, you just put me in a cab. I'm staying here with my mother. She's upstairs. And she's sick, and the doctor is with her. And he's like, we don't have any guests staying here right now.
<v Lilly Sullivan>她说：“不，你刚把我送上出租车，我跟我母亲住在这里。她就在楼上，她病了，医生正在照顾她。”他说：“我们现在没有住店的客人。”

00:01:54.590 --> 00:02:07.850
<v Lilly Sullivan>And so she runs past him. She runs upstairs. She goes to the room. And she opens the door. And the room where her mother was, it's empty. The furniture is all different. The walls are a different color. Everything is rearranged.
<v Lilly Sullivan>她从他身边跑过去，冲上楼，打开房门。她母亲住的房间现在空空如也，家具全变了，墙壁颜色也不同了，一切都重新布置过了。

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:20.530
<v Lilly Sullivan>And the hotel manager kind of chases her up. And he's like, seriously, miss, you need to leave. You can't just be in here. And so she's really confused. She starts running down the halls looking for them.
<v Lilly Sullivan>酒店经理追了上来，说：“说真的，小姐，您得离开。您不能待在这儿。”她非常困惑，开始跑遍走廊去寻找她们。

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:22.490
<v Ira Glass>Them being the doctor and her mom.
<v Ira Glass>她们指的就是那个医生和她妈妈。

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:32.650
<v Lilly Sullivan>Yeah, and so she runs out of the hotel. She runs into the street. And she just starts asking everybody. You know, I'm looking for my mom. Have you seen her?
<v Lilly Sullivan>是的，她跑出酒店，跑上街头，开始询问每个人：“我在找我妈妈，你们见过她吗？”

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:34.140
<v Ira Glass>So what happens to her?
<v Ira Glass>那她最后怎么样了？

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:38.630
<v Lilly Sullivan>Well, she just keeps looking. Years pass.
<v Lilly Sullivan>嗯，她一直找下去，岁月流逝。

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:40.210
<v Ira Glass>Oh, it's a fable.
<v Ira Glass>哦，这是一个寓言故事。

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:41.650
<v Lilly Sullivan>Right. Right.
<v Lilly Sullivan>对。对。

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:43.230
<v Ira Glass>This isn't a real story.
<v Ira Glass>这不是一个真实的故事。

00:02:43.230 --> 00:03:00.870
<v Lilly Sullivan>Right. And, you know, she's young. She's in Paris. She doesn't know anybody. And she just keeps wandering and looking. And in the eyes of everyone in Paris, she kind of becomes this madwoman wandering the streets of Paris saying, "where is my mother, where is my mother."
<v Lilly Sullivan>对。而且，你知道，她很年轻，身在巴黎，谁也不认识。她就这样一直徘徊寻找。在巴黎人眼里，她成了一个在街头游荡的疯女人，嘴里念叨着：“我妈妈在哪里，我妈妈在哪里。”

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:22.530
<v Lilly Sullivan>And sometimes, she wonders it, too. Did I invent all of this in my head? She wonders if it happened at all. But at the same time, she knows that it happened. Like, she was staying there with her mother.
<v Lilly Sullivan>有时，她自己也在想：这一切是我头脑中编造出来的吗？她怀疑这一切是否真的发生过。但同时，她又确信它发生过。毕竟，她确实曾和母亲住在那儿。

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:34.480
<v Ira Glass>Lilly, you said that this story has been stuck in your head for a while. And I know it has because I know that it was your impetus for putting together today's show. And you've been thinking about it for months, actually. Talk about why.
<v Ira Glass>莉莉，你说这个故事在你脑海里盘踞很久了。我知道它确实如此，因为我知道这就是你策划今天节目的动力。事实上，你已经想了几个月了。说说为什么吧。

00:03:34.480 --> 00:04:03.950
<v Lilly Sullivan>She goes through something in that story that I feel like I've gone through, I think that a lot of women go through all the time. And it can happen in these really small ways. They don't get talked about because they're so small. So here's an example. So years ago when I was just first getting into radio, you know, I had just graduated from this radio program. I got this little scholarship thing to go to this conference with a lot of people who have been in radio forever.
<v Lilly Sullivan>她在故事中经历的事情，我觉得我经历过，而且我觉得很多女性一直在经历。这可能以非常微小的方式发生，因为太琐碎而无人谈论。我举个例子：几年前，我刚入行做广播时，刚从一个广播课程毕业。我拿了一个小奖学金去参加一个会议，那里有很多资深的广播前辈。

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:04.090
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:12.970
<v Lilly Sullivan>And it was also, you know, one of those scholarships where we're all people of color. We're all young, in a new career. And it was all people who are older. And you know, public radio is mostly--
<v Lilly Sullivan>那也是那种针对少数族裔的奖学金项目，我们都很年轻，事业刚起步。而其他人年纪都很大。你知道，公共广播界主要是……

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:13.600
<v Ira Glass>White.
<v Ira Glass>白人。

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:21.880
<v Lilly Sullivan>Yeah. Yeah. So you're there and you're like, the young person of color who doesn't know anything about radio.
<v Lilly Sullivan>对。对。所以你站在那里，就像是一个对广播一无所知的年轻少数族裔。

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:22.700
<v Ira Glass>Right, yeah.
<v Ira Glass>没错，是的。

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:46.780
<v Lilly Sullivan>It was a little awkward or uncomfortable. And there was this older person who was a pretty big deal in radio. And he was doing this thing where he would kind of come up and talk to me like a normal person and, you know, say professional things about radio. And then he would-- you know, it's a conference. So you see people, and you walk away, and you mingle.
<v Lilly Sullivan>当时有点尴尬或不自在。有个年纪较大的广播界大人物，他走过来和我谈话，表现得像个正常人，说些专业的广播事宜。然后他……你知道，那是会议，你会看到不同的人，走开，去社交。

00:04:46.780 --> 00:05:04.570
<v Lilly Sullivan>And then he would kind of walk away. And then he would be on the other side of the room, and he would just kind of like, leer at me from a distance, kind of all the time. It felt like constantly, and he would just-- you know, he'd be waggling his eyebrows, pursing his lips. You know, like, squinting eyes.
<v Lilly Sullivan>然后他会走开。之后他在房间另一头，一直盯着我看。感觉好像是持续不断的，他会……你知道，挑动眉毛，撅起嘴唇。你知道，比如眯起眼睛。

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:05.770
<v Ira Glass>So corny.
<v Ira Glass>太俗气了。

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:25.840
<v Lilly Sullivan>I know. So every time I would turn, he would, you know, catch my eye and like raise his eyebrows again like, still here. He was messing with me. I mean, but it was weird, because then, a few minutes later, he would just walk up to me, and then start acting totally normal, as if he hadn't been doing that.
<v Lilly Sullivan>我知道。所以每次我转头，他就会捕捉到我的目光，再次挑眉，好像在说“我还在这儿”。他在逗我。但这很奇怪，因为几分钟后，他会走过来，表现得完全正常，就好像他刚才什么都没做一样。

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:26.320
<v Ira Glass>Oh, wow.
<v Ira Glass>哦，天哪。

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:45.980
<v Lilly Sullivan>He would just, oh, you're here with this program. It's a really interesting program. And it's important to cultivate new people of color in radio. And then as part of the conference, there was an event one night. And, you know, there was alcohol and, people were drinking.
<v Lilly Sullivan>他会说：“噢，你是跟着这个项目来的。这个项目很有趣，培养广播界新的少数族裔人才很重要。”会议期间有一天晚上有个活动。你知道，有酒水，人们在喝酒。

00:05:45.980 --> 00:06:02.210
<v Lilly Sullivan>And he was standing with a group of men. They're looking at me, and they're whispering. It felt like they were talking about me. I mean, they were. I could tell that they were talking about me. Someone would look at me, saying something, and then someone would laugh.
<v Lilly Sullivan>他当时和一群男人站在一起。他们看着我，窃窃私语。感觉他们在谈论我。我是说，他们确实在谈论。有人会看我一眼，说些什么，然后有人大笑。

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:04.370
<v Ira Glass>Did you have a feeling about what they were probably saying?
<v Ira Glass>你有没有感觉到他们可能在说什么？

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:27.440
<v Lilly Sullivan>I mean, I think they were just talking about like, me as a woman, how I look. Things like that. And I think that he was telling them that he was going to sleep with me, or something like that. That's the way they were looking at me. They were like, whispering to each other and squinting their eyes. And they were laughing at me.
<v Lilly Sullivan>我想他们可能是在谈论我作为女性的样子之类的。我觉得他在告诉他们他要跟我睡觉，或者类似的话。他们看我的眼神就是那样。他们凑在一起低语，眯着眼睛，对我嘲笑。

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:52.850
<v Lilly Sullivan>And then, he comes up to me and just acts totally normal, and tells me that I should come over and come meet them. You know, he'd be happy to make some introductions. They're good people to know in radio. And so I go over with him. And I like, put out my hand to meet them. And no one shook my hand. And they all just kind of laughed and looked at each other. And I just--
<v Lilly Sullivan>然后，他走过来表现得完全正常，告诉我应该过去见见他们。你知道，他很高兴为我介绍，他们是广播界值得结识的人。于是我跟他过去，我伸出手想和他们打招呼。没有人握我的手。他们只是互相看看，笑了笑，而我……

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:53.570
<v Ira Glass>Whoa.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:06:53.570 --> 00:07:24.710
<v Lilly Sullivan>Yeah, it wasn't great. It was really embarrassing. But it was so subtle. It just felt humiliating in a way that I knew would sound silly if I tried to tell someone. And then later, I talked to people about it. And they were a little skeptical. You know, they were like, those people, they wouldn't have done that, the people who make the decisions in radio. But I was like, I just, I know that it happened.
<v Lilly Sullivan>是的，感觉并不好，非常尴尬。但这一切太微妙了。那种屈辱感，我知道如果我试着告诉别人，听起来会很傻。后来，我和别人提起过，他们有点怀疑。他们说：“那些人，广播界做决策的人，不会做那种事。”但我心里清楚，我知道那确实发生了。

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:31.760
<v Ira Glass>And then in your mind, you're basically doing what the woman is doing in the story, which is like, this is definitely true. But wait, is this true?
<v Ira Glass>所以你当时脑子里基本上在做那个故事里的女人做的事，就是“这绝对是真的，但等等，这真的是真的吗？”

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:40.230
<v Lilly Sullivan>Kind of. Because, yeah, I mean, a little bit. Because, like, on the one hand, you know what it's like when people are kind of laughing at you when you put out your hand, and no one shakes it.
<v Lilly Sullivan>某种程度上。因为，是的，我稍微有一点这种感觉。因为一方面，你知道当人们在你伸出手时嘲笑你，且没人握手的感觉。

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:43.940
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, that seems pretty blatant. That seems like hard to make a mistake about.
<v Ira Glass>是的，那看起来相当明目张胆，很难产生误解。

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:45.240
<v Lilly Sullivan>It does seem blatant, right?
<v Lilly Sullivan>确实看起来很明目张胆，对吧？

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:45.540
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:07:45.540 --> 00:08:08.090
<v Lilly Sullivan>But then when people say, you must have misread everything. And the guy himself is acting like everything is totally fine. You're kind of like, is it possible that I misread everything? Am I overreacting? Did I just read all of that wrong?
<v Lilly Sullivan>但当人们说“你肯定误读了一切”，而那个人又表现得一切正常时，你就会想：有没有可能是我误读了？是我反应过度了吗？我是不是理解错了？

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:13.410
<v Ira Glass>OK, so the show that you have put together today, Lilly, was kind of inspired by that Paris story, right?
<v Ira Glass>好的，莉莉，你今天策划的节目，某种程度上就是受那个巴黎故事的启发，对吧？

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:14.840
<v Lilly Sullivan>Yeah, yeah.
<v Lilly Sullivan>是的，是的。

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:39.140
<v Ira Glass>And then we just explained to the audience that it has two stories about women. And in each of the stories, something kind of weird and unsettling happens to these women. And the world does not acknowledge how weird and wrong these things are. But in addition, unlike the woman in the Paris story and unlike you at the conference, the women in these stories, they don't think anything has gone wrong.
<v Ira Glass>我们刚刚向听众解释了，节目里有两个关于女性的故事。在每一个故事中，她们都遭遇了一些奇怪且令人不安的事情。而这个世界并不承认这些事情有多奇怪、多错误。但此外，与巴黎故事里的女人和你当时的情况不同，这些故事里的女性一开始并不觉得有什么不对劲。

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:53.810
<v Lilly Sullivan>Not at first. At first, they think everything is fine because everyone around them acts like everything is fine. And only later do they start to realize, wait, something bad did happen.
<v Lilly Sullivan>不是一开始就觉得。起初，她们认为一切都很好，因为周围的人都表现得一切正常。直到后来，她们才开始意识到：“等等，确实发生了糟糕的事情。”

00:08:53.810 --> 00:09:07.160
<v Ira Glass>And so that is going to be our show today, two stories that, I have to say, are eye-opening. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这就是我们今天的节目，两个我必须说非常发人深省的故事。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《此美国生活》。请继续收听。

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:29.540
<v Ira Glass>Act 1-- "The Old Man on My Shoulder." This first story, when the producers were working on it, started doing interviews for the story, it seemed like every single interview they came back so surprised at what they were learning. They really set out having no idea what they would find. A warning-- this story has sexual content. It's probably not right for children. And it begins with something that happened to our producer, Elna Baker.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕——“肩上的老头”。这个故事在制作人采访时，他们每次回来都对所听到的感到惊讶。他们出发时根本不知道会发现什么。警告——这个故事包含性内容，可能不适合儿童收听。故事从我们的制作人艾尔娜·贝克身上发生的事情开始。

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:47.330
<v Elna Baker>A few months ago I was talking to a guy I know, Rory, when Mormonism came up. "You grew up Mormon? You have to meet my fiancee, Reagan," he told me. "She was Mormon, too. You guys will have so much in common." I know that's like saying, you're black, meet my other black friend. But in this case, he was right.
<v Elna Baker>几个月前，我在和一位我认识的朋友罗里聊天，聊到了摩门教。“你是摩门教长大的？那你一定要见见我的未婚妻里根，”他告诉我。“她也是摩门教的，你们会很有共同语言。”我知道这听起来就像是在说“你是黑人，去见见我的另一个黑人朋友”。但在这种情况下，他是对的。

00:09:47.330 --> 00:10:11.910
<v Elna Baker>Within an hour of meeting Reagan, we were singing church songs together, like (SINGING) When I grow up, I want to be a mother and have a family. One little, two little, three little babies of my own. Of all the jobs for me, I'll choose no other. I'll have a family. Four little, five little, six little babies in my home.
<v Elna Baker>见到里根不到一小时，我们就开始一起唱教会歌曲，比如（唱）：“当我长大，我想成为母亲并拥有一个家庭。一个、两个、三个我自己的宝宝。在所有工作中，我别无他选。我会拥有一个家庭。四个、五个、六个宝宝在我的家中。”

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:20.910
<v Elna Baker>All this while Rory looked on in horror. Reagan says that Rory is constantly bowled over by the things she tells him.
<v Elna Baker>罗里在一旁惊恐地看着。里根说罗里经常被她告诉他的事情吓得目瞪口呆。

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:43.710
<v Reagan>You know, it started out kind of fun and cute. And I'd be like, you know when you're singing around a piano with your family? And he's like, no, nobody does that. That's not a normal thing for people's childhoods. So it started out kind of cute like that. And then one day, I'm like, you know when you're in an office with an old man, and the doors shut, and you're talking about sex things?
<v Reagan>你知道，一开始这听起来还有点有趣和可爱。我会说：“你知道那种和家人在钢琴旁唱歌的感觉吗？”他会说：“不，没人会那样。这不是人们童年的常态。”所以起初是可爱的。但后来有一天，我说：“你知道那种和老男人待在办公室里，门关着，谈论性话题的感觉吗？”

00:10:43.710 --> 00:11:04.590
