<v Kate>I think constantly having to account to an extraordinarily judgmental outsider about your sex life leaves an external voice. Like, you know how there's like an angel and a devil in cartoons who are always on your shoulder? I think there's always like an old man on our shoulders as Mormon women.
<v Kate>我认为不断要向一个极其挑剔的局外人汇报你的性生活，会留下一个外部的声音。你知道卡通片里总有天使和恶魔在肩上吗？我认为作为摩门教女性，我们的肩膀上总有个老男人。

00:35:58.590 --> 00:36:17.360
<v Elna Baker>This has consequences. Dr. Jennifer Finlayson-Fife is the sex therapist I talked to. She's a practicing Mormon and has had hundreds of patients who are current or former members of the church. She said some Mormon women, not all, learned from their bishop interviews to defer to an authority figure when it comes to sex.
<v Elna Baker>这产生了后果。詹妮弗·芬莱森-法伊夫博士是我采访的性治疗师。她是虔诚的摩门教徒，有数百名现任或前任教会成员患者。她说一些摩门教女性学会了在性方面顺从权威人物。

00:36:17.360 --> 00:36:32.140
<v Elna Baker>Of course, other parts of the religion reinforce that lesson, too. And these women, during sex, they think about what the man they're with wants or what their bishop would think. They don't think about what they want. This shuts their sexuality down.
<v Elna Baker>当然，宗教的其他部分也强化了这一教训。这些女性在做爱时，想的是男伴想要什么，或者主教会有什么想法。她们不会思考自己想要什么。这关闭了她们的性欲。

00:36:32.140 --> 00:37:01.970
<v Jennifer Finlayson-fife>I mean some LDS women really see it as a dangerous thing, that sex and desire in and of itself is dangerous to their goodness and dangerous to their identity as a good woman, and to their perception of the ideals of what a good LDS woman does. And so they shut it down in a more fundamental way or don't develop it in a more fundamental way. And then, the task of awakening it in marriage feels almost impossible.
<v Jennifer Finlayson-fife>有些LDS女性确实视其为危险的东西，认为性与欲望本身对她们的善良、对她们作为好女性的认同、以及对LDS好女性理想的认知是危险的。所以她们从根本上关闭了它，或者没能从根本上发展它。而结婚后唤醒它感觉几乎是不可能的。

00:37:01.970 --> 00:37:20.940
<v Elna Baker>Of course, shutting down means different things. A lot of the women I talked to said that during sex, they leave their bodies, can't even tell what they want. Rebecca was one of the women who told me she enjoys sex. But she still feels held back. She's a practicing Mormon, and she did it the right way. Married a Mormon, waited till marriage to have sex.
<v Elna Baker>当然，关闭意味着不同的东西。我交谈过的许多女性说在做爱时她们会灵魂出窍，甚至不知道自己想要什么。丽贝卡就是其中一位，她说她享受性爱，但仍感到受束缚。她是虔诚的摩门教徒，用正确的方式做了。嫁给了摩门教徒，等到结婚才发生性关系。

00:37:20.940 --> 00:37:50.620
<v Rebecca>I still sometimes will have a feeling of I'm being dirty or slutty if I enjoy this too much, or if I get too into this. Because I feel like somehow, the only pure way to have an orgasm is to be thinking about my husband, who I'm married to for eternity, and our love and worthiness before God. I don't think having those thoughts, for me, is that sexy. I don't want to think about my relationship to God.
<v Rebecca>如果我太享受它或太投入，有时依然会感到脏或淫荡。因为我觉得仿佛获得高潮的唯一纯洁方式是想着我的丈夫——我和他有永恒的婚约，还有我们在上帝面前的爱与配称。我不想在那种时刻想我和上帝的关系，那对我来说不性感。

00:37:50.620 --> 00:38:07.140
<v Elna Baker>What I've started to realize these bishop interviews did to me was I had no space for privacy of any sexual thought. So if I had a sexual thought, God was eavesdropping and heard it. I have to be like, oh, no, turn the thought off. Stop, stop, stop. This is not allowed.
<v Elna Baker>我开始意识到这些面谈对我做了什么：我没有任何性想法的隐私空间。如果我有性想法，上帝在窃听。我不得不说：“不，关闭它。停下，停下，停下。这不被允许。”

00:38:07.140 --> 00:38:22.890
<v Rebecca>Yeah. But I don't blame you for feeling that way, because that's some of our cultural and social programming. I don't think it's intentional. I don't think anybody sat there and thought, let's give these women these huge hang ups they're gonna struggle with the rest of their lives. But it happened.
<v Rebecca>是的。但我并不怪你有那种感觉，因为那是我们的文化和社会编程。我不认为这是故意的。不认为有人坐在那里想：“让我们给这些女性留下巨大的心结，让她们余生痛苦。”但它发生了。

00:38:22.890 --> 00:38:50.830
<v Elna Baker>Right, they didn't plan to give us these hangups. But they did plan to scare the bejesus out of children about sex, in a way that gave us huge hang ups. I wanted to talk to the church about this to understand how it views the bishop interviews today, after the Sam Young controversy. To see if officials really grasp how much their policies had impacted us, and if the church is rethinking these practices.
<v Elna Baker>对，他们没计划给我们留心结。但他们的确计划了要用恐吓手段让孩子对性产生恐惧，这以一种给予我们巨大心理负担的方式。我想和教会谈谈，了解在萨姆·扬争议后，他们今天如何看待面谈。看看官员们是否真的理解他们的政策对我们影响有多深，以及教会是否在反思这些做法。

00:38:50.830 --> 00:39:04.000
<v Elna Baker>LDS officials haven't given interviews on this in the past. But the director of Media Relations for the church, Eric Hawkins, agreed to talk. I told him what I'd learned from my interviewees, that these bishop interviews had stayed with us.
<v Elna Baker>LDS官员过去从未就此接受采访。但教会媒体关系主任埃里克·霍金斯同意谈一谈。我告诉他我从受访者那里学到的，这些面谈一直伴随着我们。

00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:27.970
<v Eric Hawkins>I think what you have found is a selection of individuals who have perhaps had that experience, or that feeling, whereas tens of thousands, hundreds of thousands of others have felt very differently about the process, and about-- so as I say, from my perspective, it is always heartbreaking when I hear that someone leaves that conversation not having had that experience.
<v Eric Hawkins>我认为你找到的是一小部分可能有过那种经历或感觉的人，而成千上万的人对这个过程有着非常不同的感受。所以，正如我所说，从我的角度来看，当听到有人在谈话结束后有那种不好的体验时，总是令人心碎的。

00:39:27.970 --> 00:39:31.450
<v Elna Baker>Are these questions supposed to be that explicit?
<v Elna Baker>这些问题本应如此露骨吗？

00:39:31.450 --> 00:40:02.610
<v Eric Hawkins>I think that would depend a little bit on the situation. One of the pieces of counsel that bishops are given is to not be too invasive, to adapt the conversation to the understanding and maturity of the young person who is there. And I think it's not necessary for a bishop to be overly explicit or probing in those questions. He wants to understand how that individual feels about what they have done, so that he can help apply the right amount of repentance, if you will.
<v Eric Hawkins>我认为这要取决于具体情况。给主教的一条建议是不要过于冒犯，要根据年轻人的理解能力和成熟度来调整谈话。主教没必要过于露骨或刻意盘问。他想了解个人对所作所为的感受，以便在必要时施加适当程度的悔改。

00:40:02.610 --> 00:40:25.270
<v Elna Baker>Eric says the church strongly believes that these bishop interviews with kids are a crucial part of its mission to help young people develop a close relationship with God by teaching them the standards for living a good and moral life. I pointed out to him that under the church's current guidelines, a bishop is still free to ask whatever explicit questions he wants. And inappropriate questions still seem to be happening.
<v Elna Baker>埃里克说，教会坚信主教与孩子的这些面谈是其使命的关键部分，旨在通过传授道德生活准则，帮助年轻人与上帝建立亲密关系。我向他指出，根据教会当前的指南，主教仍然可以自由提出任何他想要的露骨问题。而不恰当的问题似乎仍在发生。

00:40:25.270 --> 00:40:31.230
<v Elna Baker>I mean, I guess what's the downside to making it super clear what they can and can't ask?
<v Elna Baker>我是说，明确规定他们能问和不能问什么，有什么坏处吗？

00:40:31.230 --> 00:41:00.360
<v Eric Hawkins>Well, I think the conversation needs to be according to the understanding of that young person. You may have a young woman who is 11 years old, or 12 years old, 13 years old, who is completely innocent. You may have one of her counterparts who is of the same age, but very, very mature in her thinking, and the ways of the world, and so forth. And so the conversation would be very different for those two individuals. And that's what's outlined in the guidelines for bishops, as far as interviews.
<v Eric Hawkins>嗯，我认为谈话需要符合年轻人的理解。你可能有一个完全天真的11岁、12岁或13岁女孩，也可能有一个同龄但思想非常成熟、见多识广的女孩。所以对于这两个人，谈话会非常不同。这就是主教面谈指南中概述的内容。

00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:14.490
<v Elna Baker>In other words, bishops need the flexibility to ask whatever they think is needed. He pointed out the church did revise its guidelines for bishop interviews this year to allow parents to be in the room and to share with the parents the basic topics that they'll cover beforehand.
<v Elna Baker>换句话说，主教需要灵活性去问任何他们认为必要的问题。他指出教会确实在今年修订了面谈指南，允许家长在场，并事先与家长分享将涵盖的基本主题。

00:41:14.490 --> 00:41:18.490
<v Elna Baker>So why did you set new guidelines?
<v Elna Baker>那么，你们为什么要设定新指南呢？

00:41:18.490 --> 00:41:33.240
<v Eric Hawkins>I think this is a church that is always growing, and learning, and looking to do better. And I think there was seen an opportunity to improve the interactions between young people and bishops. And so those guidelines were set.
<v Eric Hawkins>我认为这是一个一直在成长、学习并寻求做得更好的教会。我认为人们看到了改善年轻人与主教之间互动的机会。所以设立了那些指南。

00:41:33.240 --> 00:41:38.460
<v Elna Baker>And is that because the way that questions were asked before were wrong?
<v Elna Baker>是因为以前提问的方式是错的吗？

00:41:38.460 --> 00:41:45.210
<v Eric Hawkins>No, I don't think so. I think it's a learning process. I think the way that the church is taking accountability is by constantly seeking to improve.
<v Eric Hawkins>不，我不这么认为。我认为这是一个学习过程。我认为教会负责任的方式是不断寻求改进。

00:41:45.210 --> 00:41:59.340
<v Elna Baker>You specifically said the word accountability. And I think that the church needs accountability in acknowledging that this process caused harm.
<v Elna Baker>你特别提到了“负责任”这个词。我认为教会需要承认这个过程造成了伤害，这才是负责任。

00:41:59.340 --> 00:42:06.570
<v Eric Hawkins>I think that what the church is trying to do is to constantly improve, to look for ways in which this can be made better.
<v Eric Hawkins>我认为教会正在做的是不断改进，寻找使之变得更好的途径。

00:42:06.570 --> 00:42:06.930
<v Elna Baker>Absolutely.
<v Elna Baker>绝对。

00:42:06.930 --> 00:42:07.860
<v Eric Hawkins>That those interactions can improve.
<v Eric Hawkins>使互动变得更好。

00:42:07.860 --> 00:42:36.370
<v Elna Baker>But I guess what I'm saying is in order to improve, there needs to be an admission. It feels a little like an argument I might get in with a boyfriend, or my husband, where I'm like-- so can you tell me that you did something wrong? And they're like, I'll do better. And you're like, no, but first you have to tell me you did something wrong. And then it's like, no, I'll do better. And it's like, will you just tell me, just so I know that you know that this was wrong?
<v Elna Baker>但我说的是，为了改善，需要先承认。这感觉有点像我和男友或丈夫争吵，我会说：“你能告诉我你做错了吗？”他们说：“我会做得更好。”我说：“不，首先你得承认你做错了。”然后他们说：“不，我会做得更好。”就像：“你能不能告诉我，让我知道你明白这是错的？”

00:42:36.370 --> 00:42:38.640
<v Eric Hawkins>I've had those conversations with my wife, too.
<v Eric Hawkins>我和妻子也有过那样的对话。

00:42:38.640 --> 00:42:47.730
<v Elna Baker>Uh-huh. And so do you understand what I'm asking?
<v Elna Baker>嗯哼。那么你明白我在问什么吗？

00:42:47.730 --> 00:42:49.080
<v Eric Hawkins>I do. I do.
<v Eric Hawkins>我明白，我明白。

00:42:49.080 --> 00:42:53.710
<v Elna Baker>And do you understand why it's important to me to hear that?
<v Elna Baker>那你明白为什么听到这个对我来说很重要吗？

00:42:53.710 --> 00:43:23.900
<v Eric Hawkins>Yeah. And I think, as I said, were you to come into my office as your bishop or stake president, I would sit down and council with you, and make sure you understood-- and we would understand together, why did you feel that way? What were you feeling? And how can we make you feel better? But what I can't do is go back and change your experience, your perception, your feelings that you had at that time.
<v Eric Hawkins>是的。如我所说，如果你作为我的教会成员走进我的办公室，我会坐下来建议你，确保你理解——我们一起理解，为什么你会有这种感觉？你当时感受到了什么？我们怎样才能让你感觉更好？但我无法回头改变你的经历、你的认知和你当时感受到的情绪。

00:43:23.900 --> 00:43:45.830
<v Elna Baker>Before I was baptized at the age of eight, I had to meet with the bishop. He said that I was going to be accountable for my sins from this moment on. He explained this using a dry erase board. You'll commit sins. He drew big black blobs across the white backdrop. But you can repent. He took an eraser and wiped the board until it was white again.
<v Elna Baker>在8岁受洗前，我必须见主教。他说从这一刻起，我要为我的罪负责。他用一块白板解释了这个。你会犯罪。他在白色背景上画了几个大黑点。但你可以悔改。他拿过擦子把黑点擦掉，直到白板变得洁净如新。

00:43:45.830 --> 00:44:12.470
<v Elna Baker>This was a speech a lot of Mormon kids got. Back when I was in the church, I was hooked on the feeling I got when my bishops told me I was forgiven, and clean again. I could never sit with the discomfort I felt over my sexuality. And look, I no longer believe that these men speak for God or have any authority over me. But I can't shake the feeling of wanting to be clean, to have someone else who knows tell me I'm OK.
<v Elna Baker>这是许多摩门教孩子听到的演讲。在教会时，我沉迷于主教告诉我“我被宽恕了、洁净了”的那种感觉。我永远无法面对因性欲而产生的局促感。听着，我不相信这些男人代表上帝或对我拥有任何权威。但我无法摆脱那种想变“干净”的感觉，想让一个知情者告诉我“我没问题”。

00:44:12.470 --> 00:44:49.120
<v Elna Baker>I probably should accept that there is no way that board is going to stay white. Why would I even want it to? What's so bad about drawing on it? Isn't that what it's for? But then, the second I think this, I hear another voice, "such is the way of an adulterous woman-- she eateth and wipeth her mouth and sayeth, I have done no wickedness." That's what my bishops taught me.
<v Elna Baker>我大概应该接受那块板子不可能一直洁净的。为什么我还要指望它洁净？在那上面涂画有什么坏处吗？它不就是为此存在的吗？但紧接着，当我这样想时，我听到了另一个声音：“淫妇的道，也是这样，她吃了，擦了嘴，就说：我没有行恶。”这就是我的主教教给我的。

00:44:49.120 --> 00:45:09.290
<v Ira Glass>Elna Baker is one of the producers of our show. Coming up, something that might happen to you in the hospital that you would probably be unhappy about, and you would never know that it happened. Details in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>艾尔娜·贝克是我们节目的制作人之一。接下来，在医院里可能会发生在你身上的一件事，你可能会很不高兴，却永远不会知道它发生了。细节在芝加哥公共广播电台节目继续播出后揭晓。

00:45:09.290 --> 00:45:34.120
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program-- "But That's What Happened," stories of women in situations where something unsettling happens. And sometimes, it is even hard to explain what felt wrong. And it takes a while to sort out the truth. We've arrived at Act 2 of our program, Act 2-- "While You Were Out." Lilly Sullivan has this story of something that happens to women without their consent. And they usually never find out that it happened. Here's Lilly.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《此美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目——“但这确实发生了”，讲述女性在面临令人不安的情况下的故事。有时，解释哪里不对劲甚至很困难。需要时间去理清事实。我们进入了节目的第二幕。第二幕——“当你不在时”。莉莉·沙利文讲述了这个女性在未经同意的情况下发生的事情。通常她们永远不知道。下面请听莉莉的报道。

00:45:34.120 --> 00:45:46.960
<v Lilly Sullivan>About 10 years ago, Dan Wainberg was a medical student in Canada. It was his first year working in a hospital shadowing doctors. One day he goes to his first gynecological surgery. He had never been in a gynecological operating room before.
<v Lilly Sullivan>大约10年前，丹·温伯格是加拿大的医学生。那是他在医院跟随医生实习的第一年。有一天，他去参加了人生第一次妇科手术。他以前从没进过妇科手术室。

00:45:46.960 --> 00:45:55.660
<v Dan Wainberg>So I came into the room with the patient already under a general anesthetic, so sedated, not conscious.
<v Dan Wainberg>我进入房间时，病人已经接受了全身麻醉，所以镇静了，没有意识。

00:45:55.660 --> 00:46:06.850
<v Lilly Sullivan>That's routine. The patient is usually under by the time the surgeon arrives. So the lead surgeon did a pelvic exam on her, also routine, and then turned to Dan and said, now you try.
<v Lilly Sullivan>那是例行公事。病人通常在主刀医生到达时已经睡着了。所以主刀医生给她做了盆腔检查，这也是常规，然后转头对丹说：“现在你试试。”

00:46:06.850 --> 00:46:14.110
<v Dan Wainberg>And the gynecologist sent me over to the bed to do a pelvic exam on this woman.
<v Dan Wainberg>于是妇科医生让我去床边，给这个女人做盆腔检查。

00:46:14.110 --> 00:46:39.070
<v Lilly Sullivan>Dan had done a couple pelvic exams before, but never on somebody who was unconscious. To do a pelvic exam, a doctor inserts their fingers into a patient's vagina to examine the cervix and uterus. The doctor places another hand on the abdomen and presses down trying to catch the ovaries between the hand on the abdomen and the hand in the vagina to check for abnormalities. And while Dan was doing all this, the surgeon walked away.
<v Lilly Sullivan>丹以前做过几次盆腔检查，但从未在无意识的人身上做过。盆腔检查时，医生将手指伸入病人的阴道检查子宫颈和子宫。医生将另一只手放在腹部向下压，试图在腹部和阴道的手之间触探卵巢，以检查是否有异常。而当丹做这些时，主刀医生走开了。

00:46:39.070 --> 00:47:16.060
<v Dan Wainberg>And he went off to do something else. And so I was left there by myself doing a pelvic exam on an unconscious woman as someone who really didn't have a lot of knowledge of what I'm supposed to be feeling for. And I thought to myself, who would consent to something like this? And I know that my mind wandered to, if the woman knew what was going on, you know, that she'd probably be pretty upset, and justifiably so. You know, I just thought, what am I doing? And what would this woman think if she were to wake up right now?
<v Dan Wainberg>他去干别的事了。所以我被留在那儿独自给一个无意识的女人做检查，而我作为一个人，对该感觉什么真没多少知识。我心想，谁会同意这种事？我知道我的思绪飘向了：如果这女人知道发生了什么，她肯定会很不高兴，而且完全有理由不高兴。我只是想，我在干什么？如果她现在醒来，会怎么想？

00:47:16.060 --> 00:47:19.540
<v Lilly Sullivan>As soon as this thought hit him, he stopped, walked away.
<v Lilly Sullivan>这个念头一闪而过，他停下并走开了。

00:47:19.540 --> 00:47:58.910
<v Dan Wainberg>I don't think anyone paid any attention to me whatsoever, including the surgeon who was supposed to be basically my supervisor, or teacher. After that surgery, well, I felt so uncomfortable with the initial exam that I mustered up the courage to talk to the surgeon, my supervisor. And I asked him to please make sure, you know, from now on, just make sure they know I'm here. And ask for their consent for me to do an exam. I don't want to be-- yeah, I just felt like I didn't want that on my conscience. It didn't feel good.
<v Dan Wainberg>我想没人注意我，包括那个本该是我的指导老师的主刀医生。那次手术后，我对最初的检查感到非常不安，于是鼓起勇气和主刀医生谈了谈。我请他以后一定要确保让病人知道我在场，并询问她们是否同意让我做检查。我不想……我只是觉得我不想让它压在我的良心上。感觉不好。

00:47:58.910 --> 00:48:01.900
<v Lilly Sullivan>Oh, yeah. How did he react?
<v Lilly Sullivan>噢，是啊。他有什么反应？

00:48:01.900 --> 00:48:07.420
<v Dan Wainberg>I think he just kind of brushed it off and said OK. You know-- yeah, sure.
<v Dan Wainberg>我想他只是轻描淡写地说了声“好吧”。你知道……是的，当然。

00:48:07.420 --> 00:48:17.830
<v Lilly Sullivan>The night after that first surgery, Dan was still really upset. So he called his older sister, Sarah. She was in med school, too, a year ahead. And she was like, yeah, so?
<v Lilly Sullivan>那次手术后的晚上，丹依然非常心烦。于是他给姐姐萨拉打了电话。她也是医学生，高一年级。她说：“是的，那又怎样？”

00:48:17.830 --> 00:48:34.060
<v Sara>I thought about all the times that I'd been asked to do the exact same thing. And when it happened to me, I didn't really think twice about it. I actually thought, oh, this is a fantastic learning opportunity. Because I found learning the pelvic exam a little awkward also.
<v Sara>我回想起自己所有被要求做同样事情的经历。当它发生在我身上时，我没想两次。我甚至觉得：噢，这是一个很棒的学习机会。因为我发现学习盆腔检查也稍微有点尴尬。

00:48:34.060 --> 00:48:47.770
<v Sara>So when the surgeon invited me to also do a pelvic exam, I thought, great. I'd love to. And I don't think it had ever occurred to me even once that I might be doing something unethical. It just seemed like a normal part of practice.
<v Sara>所以当外科医生邀请我也做一次检查时，我觉得：太好了，我很乐意。我想我甚至从未想过我可能在做不道德的事。这看起来只是实习的一个正常部分。

00:48:47.770 --> 00:49:09.190
<v Lilly Sullivan>It was kind of normal. Pelvic exams are hard to learn. Frankly, under the best of circumstances, they're uncomfortable, awkward. So for years in teaching hospitals, a common way to train students was that when someone was unconscious during a gynecological surgery, the head surgeon would do the exam and then a student would repeat the exam for practice.
<v Lilly Sullivan>这确实算正常。盆腔检查很难学。坦白说，在最好的情况下，它们也让人不适、尴尬。所以多年来在教学医院里，培训学生的一种常见方式是，当有人在妇科手术麻醉期间无意识时，主刀医生会做检查，然后学生会为了练习重复检查一次。

00:49:09.190 --> 00:49:28.360
<v Lilly Sullivan>As far as consent goes, whenever anyone goes to a hospital, they sign an overall release form. At a teaching hospital, which lots of hospitals are, there's a part on the form that says something like, I acknowledge that medical students may be part of my treatment team. That's what a teaching hospital is, teaching people how to do stuff.
<v Lilly Sullivan>至于同意，任何人去医院都要签署一份综合发布表。在教学医院（很多医院都是），表上会有一栏写着类似：“我承认医学生可能是我的治疗团队成员。”这就是教学医院的本质，教人怎么做事。

00:49:28.360 --> 00:49:41.780
<v Lilly Sullivan>These kinds of exams aren't just a thing in Canada. They're common in the US, too. Rectal exams happen the same way for men while they're unconscious during prostate surgery. And the more Sara thought about it, she started to feel weird, too.
<v Lilly Sullivan>这些检查不仅在加拿大，在美国也很常见。男性在前列腺手术中无意识时，直肠检查也是同样进行的。萨拉越想越觉得奇怪。

00:49:41.780 --> 00:49:54.970
<v Sara>I think, actually, I probably felt a little bit embarrassed. So my first reaction was just to say, oh, what did you do? But in my head, I was thinking, oh, man. Why haven't I ever thought about this before?
<v Sara>我想其实我当时可能感到有点尴尬。所以第一反应只是问“你做了什么？”但在脑海里，我在想：天哪，我为什么从没想过这件事？

00:49:54.970 --> 00:50:00.190
<v Lilly Sullivan>And then how did you feel about that reaction now? Do you have a different feeling about your initial--
<v Lilly Sullivan>那你现在对那个反应感觉如何？你对最初的……有不同的感觉吗？

00:50:00.190 --> 00:50:23.740
<v Sara>Oh, yeah, no, I think it's horrifying, absolutely. I think, like, what's wrong with me? Why didn't this occur to me that I hadn't actually met this woman before? And it never occurred to me. And I think that's pretty horrible. Yeah, it's one of those dirty little secrets of medicine.
<v Sara>噢，当然，我觉得这太可怕了，绝对的。我想：“我怎么了？为什么我没意识到我根本没见过这个女人？”这件事从未闪过我的念头。我觉得这太可怕了。是的，这是医学界那些阴暗的小秘密之一。

00:50:23.740 --> 00:50:42.340
<v Lilly Sullivan>After they hung up, Sara kept thinking about it, obsessing over the women, the patients getting these pelvic exams. Do they know that this might happen under anesthesia? And if not, how they feel about it? Sarah had to do a research project for school that year. And she wanted to look at this.
<v Lilly Sullivan>挂断后，萨拉一直想着这件事，痴迷于那些接受盆腔检查的女性患者。她们知道麻醉下可能会发生这种事吗？如果不知道，她们会怎么想？萨拉那年要为学校做一个研究课题，她想调查这个。

00:50:42.340 --> 00:51:01.000
<v Sara>I specifically started talking to my boyfriend about it. Because he had wanted to be my research partner. And I said, oh, I've got this great idea. Let's do our research project on pelvic exams that are done without consent. And he said, no way. I do not want to be your research partner for that.
<v Sara>我特别开始和男友谈论这件事。因为他想做我的研究伙伴。我说：“噢，我有个好主意，我们来做关于‘未经同意的盆腔检查’的研究课题吧。”他说：“绝对不行，我才不要做这个的研究伙伴。”

00:51:01.000 --> 00:51:03.190
<v Lilly Sullivan>Why?
<v Lilly Sullivan>为什么？

00:51:03.190 --> 00:51:19.160
<v Sara>He felt that the pelvic exam under anesthesia is an incredibly important learning opportunity for male students. And he thought that even by looking at this issue, we were opening up a big can of worms. And he didn't want to have anything to do with it. He thought it was wrong that I was even looking into this issue.
<v Sara>他觉得麻醉下的盆腔检查对男学生来说是极其重要的学习机会。他认为即使审视这个问题，我们也在打开一个潘多拉魔盒。他不想和这件事有任何瓜葛。他觉得我调查这个问题本身就是错的。

00:51:19.160 --> 00:51:20.420
<v Lilly Sullivan>Oh, wow.
<v Lilly Sullivan>哦，天哪。

00:51:20.420 --> 00:51:20.680
<v Sara>Yeah.
<v Sara>是的。

00:51:20.680 --> 00:51:21.200
<v Lilly Sullivan>So you guys had some--
<v Lilly Sullivan>所以你们……

00:51:21.200 --> 00:51:23.300
<v Sara>We were not a match made in heaven.
<v Sara>我们不是天造地设的一对。

00:51:23.300 --> 00:51:25.760
<v Sara>[LAUGHTER]
<v Sara>（笑声）

00:51:25.760 --> 00:51:38.740
<v Lilly Sullivan>He never came around on that one. Sara found that most of her classmates had done exams like this, practice exams on anesthetized women. And lots of them had never really thought about it either until Sara pointed it out to them.
<v Lilly Sullivan>他始终没转过弯来。萨拉发现她的大多数同学都做过这种检查，在麻醉的女性身上练习检查。许多人也从未真正思考过它，直到萨拉指出来。

00:51:38.740 --> 00:51:42.270
<v Lilly Sullivan>Did you notice that women thought something different from men or was it all--
<v Lilly Sullivan>你有没有注意到女性和男性的想法不同，还是说……

00:51:42.270 --> 00:51:57.460
<v Sara>Yes. Yes, I think a lot of men felt the same way my boyfriend did, that this is how we're going to learn. Because women don't always let us do this when they're awake. So we need to learn while they're asleep. For sure.
<v Sara>是的，是的。我觉得很多男性和我的男友感觉一样，认为这就是我们学习的方式。因为女性不总是在清醒时让我们做这个，所以我们需要在她们睡着时学习。确实如此。

00:51:57.460 --> 00:52:12.280
<v Lilly Sullivan>The next year, she decided to survey a bunch of female patients at her teaching hospital and see what they said. Did they know that medical students might do exams? How did the women feel about it? Do patients want to be asked for consent beforehand? No one had ever studied that before.
<v Lilly Sullivan>第二年，她决定调查她所在的教学医院的一群女性病人，看看她们怎么说。她们知道可能有医学生参与检查吗？女性对此感觉如何？病人希望事先被询问是否同意吗？以前从没人研究过这一点。

00:52:12.280 --> 00:52:32.410
<v Sara>At the time, I was like, come on, what's the big deal? Let's ask the question and find out what the answer is. I thought it's about time somebody really answers the question-- if you ask women, are they going to say yes? Right? Are they going to say yes or are they going to say no? That was the question that nobody had asked yet.
<v Sara>那时我想：“得了吧，这有什么大不了的？让我们问问，看答案是什么。”我觉得现在是有人真正回答这个问题的时候了——如果你问女性，她们会说愿意吗？对吧？她们会同意还是拒绝？这是以前没人问过的问题。

00:52:32.410 --> 00:52:56.170
<v Lilly Sullivan>Here's what Sara and her research partners found. They polled 102 female surgery patients at their teaching hospital's pelvic floor disorder center. So that's women who are likely to have had pelvic surgery or who were likely to have it in the near future. The vast majority, 81% of them, had no idea that a med student might do a pelvic exam on them while they were asleep. And most said, yeah, they wanted to be asked.
<v Lilly Sullivan>这是萨拉和她的研究伙伴发现的。她们调查了盆腔修复中心102名女性手术病人。这些女性很可能做过或即将做盆腔手术。绝大多数（81%）不知道医学生可能会在她们睡着时做盆腔检查。大多数人表示：是的，她们希望被征求意见。

00:52:56.170 --> 00:53:19.050
<v Lilly Sullivan>And here's the most surprising thing they found. 53% of women said they actually wouldn't mind being examined by a student while unconscious as long as someone asked them for permission beforehand. So a real find-- women would say yes. All you had to do was ask. So just ask, right? Problem solved.
<v Lilly Sullivan>最令人惊讶的是：53%的女性表示，只要事先征得允许，她们实际上不介意在无意识时由学生进行检查。所以，真正的结论是：女性会同意的。只要问一下就行了。只需询问，对吧？问题解决了。

00:53:19.050 --> 00:53:45.610
<v Lilly Sullivan>After Sara and her co-authors published their research, people flipped out. The Globe and Mail wrote an article about it. People were shocked to learn that this was something that went on at all. Soon afterward, the medical guidelines in Canada changed. The Society of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, also the Association of Professors of Obstetrics and Gynecology revised their positions. They said that doctor should always ask for specific consent for this kind of exam.
<v Lilly Sullivan>在萨拉和她的合著者发表研究后，人们炸锅了。《环球邮报》写了一篇报道，人们对这种事情竟然会发生感到震惊。很快，加拿大的医疗准则变了。妇产科医师协会和妇产科教授协会修正了立场，称医生应始终就此类检查寻求明确同意。

00:53:45.610 --> 00:54:07.160
<v Lilly Sullivan>In the US, the American Medical Association, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, and the Association of American Medical Colleges, they've also condemned this kind of pelvic exam. It's illegal in five states, Virginia, California, Hawaii, Illinois, and Oregon. A lot of teaching hospitals say they don't do it anymore, that it's a thing of the past.
<v Lilly Sullivan>在美国，美国医学会、妇产科医师学会和医学院协会也谴责了此类检查。它在五个州——弗吉尼亚、加利福尼亚、夏威夷、伊利诺伊和俄勒冈——是违法的。许多教学医院说他们不再这样做了，这已成过去式。

00:54:07.160 --> 00:54:24.400
<v Lilly Sullivan>But medical students say it still happens. A couple of years ago, a biomedical ethicist named Phoebe Friesen had just started teaching medical ethics at Mount Sinai Medical School in New York. The students who were following doctors in hospitals had to talk about ethical dilemmas in medicine.
<v Lilly Sullivan>但医学生们说它依然在发生。几年前，生物伦理学家菲比·弗里森刚开始在纽约西奈山医学院教授医学伦理学。那些跟随医生实习的学生必须谈论医学中的伦理困境。

00:54:24.400 --> 00:54:51.580
<v Phoebe Friesen>And when I was working with students who were in their OB-GYN clerkship, a lot of them brought up this practice and the fact that they'd been asked to perform pelvic exams on women under anesthetic who hadn't consented. And this one came up all the time, in nearly every session that I did. And generally, everyone was often sort of seeking permission or consensus that this is OK.
<v Phoebe Friesen>当我和正在实习妇产科的学生们交流时，他们很多人提到了这种做法，以及他们被要求在未征得同意的情况下给麻醉下的女性做盆腔检查的事实。几乎每次讨论都会出现这个问题。总的来说，大家都在寻求允许或共识，觉得这是可以的。

00:54:51.580 --> 00:54:55.360
<v Lilly Sullivan>Wow, so all the students had experienced this?
<v Lilly Sullivan>哇，所以所有学生都经历过？

00:54:55.360 --> 00:55:15.400
<v Phoebe Friesen>Yeah, so I think as long as they were participating in gynecological surgeries, it was really the norm. But I think a lot of them felt kind of confused or maybe ashamed. I think a lot of them were seeking reassurance that that was OK. And I just ended up talking to so many who felt uncomfortable.
<v Phoebe Friesen>是的，只要他们参与妇科手术，这真的就是常态。但我认为他们很多人感到困惑，甚至羞耻。他们很多人在寻求某种“那是没关系的”安慰。而我最终和太多感到不舒服的学生交谈过。

00:55:15.400 --> 00:55:28.740
<v Lilly Sullivan>They were doing these pelvic exams at a bunch of different hospitals around New York City. Phoebe had never heard anything about these kinds of exams. She was horrified. She started asking everyone, random students she'd meet at conferences, parties.
<v Lilly Sullivan>他们在纽约市的很多医院做这些盆腔检查。菲比以前从未听说过这些检查，她感到震惊。她开始询问所有人，她在会议上、派对上见到的随机学生。

00:55:28.740 --> 00:55:48.370
<v Lilly Sullivan>And she found that students had experienced it everywhere, at hospitals all over the country. Because teaching hospitals take a lot of patients without insurance or who are on Medicaid, these practice exams end up being done disproportionately on poor women, women of color, homeless women. Phoebe was surprised to learn there are a lot of people who are still in favor of it.
<v Lilly Sullivan>她发现学生们到处都经历过，在全国各地的医院里。因为教学医院接收很多没有保险或领取医疗补助的病人，这些练习检查最终不成比例地针对贫困女性、少数族裔女性、无家可归的女性。菲比很惊讶地发现仍有很多人支持它。

00:55:48.370 --> 00:55:59.980
<v Phoebe Friesen>And I think especially there was a lot of men who were dismissive, and there was a lot of people from within the medical community. Or a lot of people would say things like, well, what you don't know can't hurt you.
<v Phoebe Friesen>而且我觉得尤其是很多男性对此不屑一顾，医学界内部也有很多人持这种态度。很多人会说：“你不知道就不会受伤。”

00:55:59.980 --> 00:56:00.700
<v Lilly Sullivan>Oh my god.
<v Lilly Sullivan>天哪。

00:56:00.700 --> 00:56:06.410
<v Sara>That kind of response, which I felt like was really, really weird.
<v Sara>那种回应，我觉得真的很奇怪。

00:56:06.410 --> 00:56:26.530
<v Lilly Sullivan>So she decided to dissect the ethics of it and put together an article for the Journal of Bioethics. It's one of the leading journals in the field. She parsed out five common arguments in support of examining unconscious patients, evaluated each of them, and concluded that the practice is unethical. The response from doctors? Not so good.
<v Lilly Sullivan>于是她决定剖析其中的伦理，为《生物伦理学杂志》撰写了一篇文章，那是该领域领先的杂志之一。她解析了五个支持检查无意识病人的常见论点，一一评估，并断定该做法是不道德的。医生的反应？并不怎么好。

00:56:26.530 --> 00:56:53.920
<v Lilly Sullivan>Some said these exams are necessary for teaching. Some said these exams never happen, which, of course, can't both be true. One argument Phoebe has heard a lot, the vagina is just a body part. . Be a professional. Don't be a prude. She calls this the "is the vagina different from the mouth" objection. Sara heard this one a lot when she was doing her research. Here's Sara.
<v Lilly Sullivan>有人说这些检查对教学是必要的，有人说这些检查根本不会发生——当然两者不可能同时为真。菲比听到过很多的一种论点是：“阴道只是个身体部位……专业点。别当个道貌岸然的人。”她称之为“阴道与嘴有何不同”的反驳。萨拉做研究时也经常听到。请听萨拉的说法。

00:56:53.920 --> 00:57:07.700
<v Sara>So who cares? This is just another exam. This is just another thing that medical students do in the hospital. Why is it different than looking in someone's mouth, or looking at their hip? How is this different?
<v Sara>所以谁在乎呢？这只是另一个检查而已。这只是医学生在医院做的另一件事。它和看别人的嘴或臀部有什么不同？它有什么区别？

00:57:07.700 --> 00:57:09.350
<v Lilly Sullivan>How is it different?
<v Lilly Sullivan>有什么区别？

00:57:09.350 --> 00:57:27.490
<v Sara>The thing that really makes it different is not what we, as doctors, think about it. The thing that makes it different is what the patients think about it. And if you ask women, they think it's different. So it's different, yeah.
<v Sara>真正让它不同的不是我们作为医生怎么看。真正让它不同的，是病人们怎么看。如果你问女性，她们认为不同。所以，它就是不同。

00:57:27.490 --> 00:57:43.990
<v Sara>For me, I would want to know if somebody was going to be examining my vagina while I was asleep. Absolutely, I would like to know. I mean, I've been in that situation. I've been there. I've been that crying woman waiting to go into surgery. So I understand what it feels like.
<v Sara>对我来说，如果有人要在我不省人事时检查我的阴道，我会想知道的。绝对会。我经历过那种情况。我去过那里。我当过那个等着进手术室哭泣的女人。所以我理解那种感觉。

00:57:43.990 --> 00:57:48.440
<v Lilly Sullivan>Sara has had gynecological surgery under anesthesia. After a miscarriage.
<v Lilly Sullivan>萨拉在流产后，曾有过麻醉下做妇科手术的经历。

00:57:48.440 --> 00:58:15.460
<v Sara>I've been in an operating room, asleep. You're asleep in a cold operating room with your feet up in the air, and a bunch of strangers around you. And you're exposed for all these people to see. It's very emotional. Yeah, it is. Yeah, because you're very vulnerable when you're asleep. I mean, before I delivered a baby, they asked me if I wanted a med student or a resident in the room. So I think if I'm asleep, I should be offered the same courtesy.
<v Sara>我在手术室睡着过。你睡在一个冰冷的手术室里，双脚吊在半空，周围是一群陌生人。你处于暴露状态，所有人都看着你。这非常感性。是的，确实。因为你在睡着时非常脆弱。我是说，生孩子前，他们问我是否想要医学生或住院医在场。所以我想如果我睡着了，我也应该得到同样的礼遇。

00:58:15.460 --> 00:58:43.970
<v Lilly Sullivan>For this story, I wanted to talk to women who have been examined under anesthesia without their consent. But I couldn't find anyone. These exams don't become part of a woman's medical records. They don't go into their charts at the hospital. And of course, this happens while they're unconscious. So pretty much, by definition, anyone who's gone through this will probably never know.
<v Lilly Sullivan>为了这个故事，我想采访那些在未经同意下接受麻醉检查的女性，但我找不到人。这些检查不会记录在病人的医疗记录里，不会进入医院的图表。当然，这发生在她们无意识时。所以基本上，经历过这些的人可能永远都不会知道。

00:58:43.970 --> 00:58:46.480
<v Ira Glass>Lilly Sullivan.
<v Ira Glass>莉莉·沙利文。

00:58:46.480 --> 00:58:47.480
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC -- "SISTERS UNARMED" BY 79.5]
<v Ira Glass>（音乐——79.5乐团《姐妹们缴械》）

00:58:47.480 --> 00:59:09.460
<v Ira Glass>(SINGING) Why should I change 'cause the world is evil? Why rearrange the way that I am?
<v Ira Glass>（唱）为什么要改变，只因世界邪恶？为什么要重组那个本真的我？

